claire hattrick would like to go back on  hrt to ease menopausal joint pain but  doesn't want to start medication she  can't be sure she can continue  she says some women are traveling miles  to source hrt but limiting some  prescriptions to three months to tackle  supply issues is too little too late  i know women  in my group they're messaging me  constantly on clipboard claire you know  i can't get hold of my hrt i'm cutting  patches in half you know women are  trading drugs in car parks and i think  women are that desperate they do  whatever it takes i think they would pay  through the nose for it which is not  it's not acceptable women shouldn't have  to be buying off the black market and  then you've got the worry is do you  really know what you're taking  hrt can help alleviate severe menopause  symptoms including hot flushes night  sweats and anxiety  but of the treatments the government  wants to restrict this birmingham  pharmacist told us two are not available  and one has limited supply you have to  say i'm sorry but i can't get hold of  this you know or if they bring in a  person we have to check on a daily basis  so if they come in with a prescription i  have to tell them wait here let me check  the stock levels before you leave you  know because i just don't want you to  you know leave a prescription here  expect that i'm going to fill it for you  lynn robinson is the lead consultant at  birmingham women's hospital for  menopause and fertility and is also on  the medical advisory council for the  british menopause society she says there  needs to be longer-term solutions the  department of health and social care  have previously allowed pharmacists to  prescribe to change their prescriptions  to something equivalent if  whatever the gp or doctor's prescribed  isn't in stock  and um  do you think that will solve the problem  it's not solve the problem but it'll  make things easier the government says  it's listening to women's concerns and  will do everything in its power to  increase access to hrt but campaigners  say supply is outstripping demand after  an explosion in the use of hrt by women  struggling with the menopause lisa  holland sky news
 

